    tag: 'Guide',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    contentHtml: "<p>The fastest way to add captions to a YouTube video is to upload an SRT file directly in YouTube Studio. YouTube&apos;s built-in auto-captions are convenient, but they sit at around 80% accuracy on clean audio — and drop fast with accents, technical terms, or background noise. An uploaded SRT file gives you full control and indexes better in YouTube search.</p><h3>Auto-Captions vs. Uploaded SRT: What&apos;s the Difference?</h3><p>YouTube auto-captions use Google&apos;s speech recognition and generate captions automatically after you upload a video. They&apos;re free and zero effort, but they make errors you&apos;ll want to fix — wrong names, missed punctuation, run-on sentences with no breaks.</p><p>An uploaded SRT file is a caption file you create separately and attach to the video. You control every word, every line break, and every timestamp. YouTube also indexes the text from uploaded captions more reliably, which means your video is more likely to appear in search results for spoken keywords.</p><div class='overflow-x-auto'><table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Auto-Captions</th><th>Uploaded SRT</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>~80% (clean audio)</td><td>Up to 98.5% with AI transcription</td></tr><tr><td>Editing control</td><td>Manual fixes only</td><td>Full control before upload</td></tr><tr><td>Search indexing</td><td>Inconsistent</td><td>Reliable</td></tr><tr><td>Supports translations</td><td>Limited</td><td>Yes, upload per language</td></tr><tr><td>Time investment</td><td>None</td><td>5–15 min with the right tool</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Step 1: Generate an SRT File from Your Video</h3><p>Before you touch YouTube Studio, you need an SRT file. The quickest way is to run your video through <a href='/video-to-subtitles'>VideoText&apos;s video-to-subtitles tool</a>. Paste your YouTube URL or upload the file directly — it uses Whisper large-v3 and returns a timed SRT in under a few minutes for most videos.</p><p>If your captions need timing tweaks or grammar fixes before export, run them through <a href='/fix-subtitles'>Fix Subtitles</a> first. It catches common issues like overlapping timestamps, lines that are too long, and missing punctuation.</p><p>Need captions in another language? <a href='/translate-subtitles'>Translate Subtitles</a> supports 50+ languages and keeps the original timing intact — useful if your audience is spread across regions.</p><h3>Step 2: Upload the SRT File in YouTube Studio</h3><p>Once you have your SRT file, here&apos;s exactly how to attach it to a YouTube video:</p><ol><li>Go to <a href='https://studio.youtube.com' target='_blank' rel='noopener noreferrer'>studio.youtube.com</a> and sign in.</li><li>Click <strong>Content</strong> in the left sidebar, then find the video you want to caption.</li><li>Click the pencil icon (Edit) next to the video title.</li><li>In the video details page, scroll down and click <strong>Subtitles</strong> in the left menu.</li><li>Click the <strong>Add Language</strong> button and select your caption language.</li><li>Under the language row, click <strong>Add</strong> next to &quot;Subtitles.&quot;</li><li>Choose <strong>Upload file</strong>, then select <strong>With timing</strong> (since your SRT already has timestamps).</li><li>Select your .srt file from your computer and click <strong>Upload and publish</strong>.</li></ol><p>YouTube processes the file in seconds. Refresh the Subtitles page and you&apos;ll see your language listed with a &quot;Published&quot; status. The captions go live on the video immediately.</p><h3>Step 3: Verify the Captions Look Right</h3><p>Open your video in a regular browser tab (not Studio) and turn on captions using the CC button. Watch at least 2–3 minutes — pay attention to speaker changes, proper nouns, and any timestamps that feel early or late.</p><p>If something&apos;s off, go back to your SRT file, fix the lines, and re-upload. YouTube lets you replace caption files without re-uploading the video. It takes 30 seconds.</p><h3>Why This Actually Matters for Search</h3><p>YouTube is the second-largest search engine in the world. The text in your captions is one of the signals it uses to understand what your video is about. Auto-captions are not always indexed — YouTube&apos;s documentation confirms this varies by account and region. Uploaded captions are indexed consistently.</p><p>That means if someone searches for a specific phrase you say at 4:32 in your video, a clean uploaded SRT increases the chance your video surfaces. It also improves watch time for viewers who watch without sound — which YouTube&apos;s algorithm treats as a positive signal.</p><ul><li><strong>Deaf and hard-of-hearing viewers</strong> rely on accurate captions — auto-captions regularly fail them.</li><li><strong>Non-native speakers</strong> follow along better with correct text on screen.</li><li><strong>Viewers in public places</strong> (phones on mute) stay engaged instead of dropping off.</li><li><strong>Repurposed content</strong> — your SRT file can become a blog post, show notes, or a transcript page with almost no extra work.</li></ul><p>If you&apos;re managing a channel with a backlog of videos, <a href='/batch-process'>batch processing</a> lets you generate SRT files for up to 100 videos at once instead of doing them one by one.</p><h3>Get Started</h3><p>The whole process — from raw video to published captions — takes under 15 minutes the first time and faster once you&apos;ve done it once. Start by generating your SRT file: paste your YouTube URL into <a href='/video-to-subtitles'>VideoText&apos;s subtitle tool</a>, download the file, and follow the upload steps above. Your video will have accurate, searchable captions before the end of the hour.</p>",
