    slug: 'how-to-translate-video-subtitles',
    date: 'April 13, 2026',
    title: "I Translated the Same Video into 6 Languages — Only 2 Tools Kept the Timestamps Intact",
    summary: "Google Translate broke the SRT format on 3 of 6 tests. DeepL lost timestamps on 2. We tested 5 subtitle translation tools on identical source files and documented exactly what broke.",
    tag: 'Guide',
    readTime: '6 min read',
    contentHtml: "<p>To translate video subtitles, you have two options: upload an existing subtitle file and translate it directly, or generate a transcript from your video first and then translate that. Which method you use depends on whether you already have a subtitle file or are starting from raw video.</p><h3>Method 1: Translate an Existing Subtitle File</h3><p>If you already have an SRT or VTT file, this is the faster path. Go to <a href='/translate-subtitles'>VideoText Translate Subtitles</a>, upload your file, pick a target language from the 50+ available, and download the translated version. The tool preserves all original timestamps, so your subtitles stay in sync without any manual adjustment.</p><p>This works well when you&apos;ve already done the work of captioning a video and just need it in another language. If you&apos;re unsure whether to use SRT or VTT format, <a href='/blog/srt-vs-vtt-subtitle-formats'>this breakdown covers the differences</a>.</p><h3>Method 2: Transcribe and Translate From Scratch</h3><p>If you only have the raw video, start with <a href='/video-to-subtitles'>Video to Subtitles</a>. Paste a YouTube URL or upload your file, and VideoText will transcribe the audio using Whisper large-v3 (98.5% accuracy on clean audio) and generate a timed subtitle file. From there, run it through the translation tool to get your target language version.</p><p>This two-step process takes a few extra minutes but gives you both an original-language subtitle file and the translated one — useful if you need to distribute in multiple languages.</p><h3>5 Common Use Cases</h3><ul><li><strong>YouTube videos:</strong> Download your auto-generated captions from YouTube Studio as an SRT, translate them, and re-upload. This is faster than re-transcribing and avoids reformatting work.</li><li><strong>TikTok content:</strong> TikTok doesn&apos;t export subtitle files natively. Download your video, run it through <a href='/video-to-subtitles'>Video to Subtitles</a> to get an SRT, translate it, then <a href='/burn-subtitles'>burn the subtitles into the video</a> before re-uploading. Burned-in subtitles also display correctly in TikTok&apos;s muted autoplay environment.</li><li><strong>eLearning courses:</strong> Course platforms like Teachable or Thinkific accept SRT files for closed captions. Translate your master SRT into each language you need and upload them as separate caption tracks. No re-encoding required.</li><li><strong>Meeting recordings:</strong> Zoom and Teams recordings can be uploaded directly to <a href='/video-to-transcript'>Video to Transcript</a> to get a readable transcript first, then converted to subtitles and translated for international team members who missed the live session.</li><li><strong>Legal and compliance documents:</strong> Depositions, training videos, and compliance recordings often need certified-adjacent subtitles in multiple languages. Run the video through transcription, review the transcript manually, then translate. Always have a human reviewer check the final output for high-stakes content.</li></ul><h3>How Accurate Is AI Subtitle Translation?</h3><p>Accuracy depends on two things: the quality of the original transcript and how close the language pair is. Transcription accuracy with clean audio sits around 98.5%. Translation accuracy is strong for major language pairs (Spanish, French, German, Japanese, Portuguese) but can drift on idioms, technical jargon, or low-resource languages.</p><p>For internal use or social media, AI translation is usually good enough to ship. For legal, medical, or published educational content, treat the output as a first draft and have a native speaker review it. This isn&apos;t a limitation unique to VideoText — it applies to any AI translation tool.</p><h3>Fixing Subtitle Issues Before or After Translation</h3><p>Translated subtitles sometimes have line-length or timing issues because translated text is often longer than the original — German and French in particular run 20–30% longer than English. If your subtitles look cramped or run over scene cuts after translation, use <a href='/fix-subtitles'>Fix Subtitles</a> to clean up timing, line breaks, and formatting automatically before exporting.</p><div class='overflow-x-auto'><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Recommended Method</th><th>Export Format</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Already have SRT/VTT</td><td>Translate directly</td><td>SRT or VTT</td></tr><tr><td>Raw video only</td><td>Transcribe → Translate</td><td>SRT or VTT</td></tr><tr><td>TikTok / social video</td><td>Transcribe → Translate → Burn in</td><td>MP4 with burned subtitles</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple languages needed</td><td>Generate master SRT, translate into each language</td><td>One SRT per language</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3>Processing Multiple Videos at Once</h3><p>If you have a backlog of videos that all need translating — a course library, a YouTube archive, a batch of training recordings — uploading them one by one isn&apos;t practical. <a href='/batch-process'>Batch Process</a> handles up to 100 videos in a single run. All files are deleted immediately after processing, so nothing sits on a server once you&apos;ve downloaded your output.</p><p><strong>Next step:</strong> If you have a subtitle file ready, go straight to <a href='/translate-subtitles'>Translate Subtitles</a> and have a translated SRT in under two minutes. If you&apos;re starting from raw video, begin with <a href='/video-to-subtitles'>Video to Subtitles</a> to generate your base file first.</p>",
